    "excerpt": "A stable chair can be more romantic than an elaborate setup.",
    "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Seated intimacy can emphasize face-to-face attention and reduce the pressure to move constantly. The goal is not to reproduce a pose from a photograph; it is to choose an arrangement that suits the people involved.</p><h2 id=\"try-this\">A practical idea</h2><p>Try a side-by-side seat for handholding, facing one another for a conversation, or sharing a supportive sofa for a cuddle. Check that furniture is stable, feet have support, and neither person feels trapped.</p><h2 id=\"real-life\">What it might look like</h2><p>After a tiring day, a couple chooses to sit facing each other with tea and share three things they appreciated that week. The quieter format feels more connected than a planned elaborate date.</p><h2 id=\"boundaries\">Boundaries and wellbeing</h2><p>Respect personal space and access needs. A person who uses mobility equipment should lead decisions about positioning and assistance. Ask before moving someone or their equipment.</p><h2 id=\"conversation\">A question to take with you</h2><p>What seating arrangement helps you feel relaxed enough to be present?</p><p>Neither person owes participation, disclosure, or physical intimacy. Preferences can change. Ask, listen, and make it easy to decline.</p><h2 id=\"deeper-context\">Choose stability first</h2><p>A chair or sofa should support the person using it without wobbling or requiring awkward balancing. Foot support, armrests, and space for mobility equipment can matter more than the appearance of the furniture. Ask the person what setup works for them instead of adjusting their position without permission.</p><h2 id=\"practical-framework\">Make the moment personal</h2><p>Seated connection can be a shared playlist, holding hands, a quiet conversation, or an optional embrace. If you want to sit closer, ask first. If one person prefers more space, keep the conversation going without making proximity a measure of affection.</p><h2 id=\"real-world-decisions\">Accessibility is collaboration</h2><p>Mobility, pain, and fatigue can change from day to day. Rather than assuming what a partner can do, ask what would make the experience easier. A plan that accommodates the people involved is more successful than one that imitates a picture or prescribed pose.</p><h2 id=\"small-adjustments\">Small adjustments that matter</h2><p>Choose furniture that supports both people independently. If one person needs a higher seat, a footrest, or room for a mobility aid, build the arrangement around that need. A seated conversation can be intimate without touch; try facing each other with a drink and leaving enough space to shift positions.</p>",
