    "art": "moon",
    "excerpt": "Build excitement with kindness, not uncertainty.",
    "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Anticipation can be created by having something pleasant to look forward to: a favorite meal, a planned date, a thoughtful note, or a conversation about a shared wish. It should not depend on withholding affection or manufacturing insecurity.</p><h2 id=\"try-this\">A practical idea</h2><p>Plan a surprise with clear boundaries. Ask whether your partner enjoys surprises, then choose from agreed categories: food, music, activities, or time together. Share enough details that they can opt out comfortably.</p><h2 id=\"real-life\">What it might look like</h2><p>One partner leaves a note hinting at a living-room picnic. The other can choose a different night without being made to feel ungrateful.</p><h2 id=\"boundaries\">Boundaries and wellbeing</h2><p>A surprise is not consent. Never spring sexual activities, guests, recording, or public exposure on someone. The best anticipation is one that remains enjoyable even if plans change.</p><h2 id=\"conversation\">A question to take with you</h2><p>What is one surprise you would genuinely enjoy, and one you would rather avoid?</p><p>Neither person owes participation, disclosure, or physical intimacy. Preferences can change. Ask, listen, and make it easy to decline.</p><h2 id=\"deeper-context\">Anticipation is not uncertainty</h2><p>Healthy anticipation comes from looking forward to something pleasant, not wondering whether affection will be withheld or a boundary tested. A planned evening can still hold surprises in the details. Let the other person know the broad category, timing, and any practical requirements.</p><h2 id=\"practical-framework\">Try the three-clue invitation</h2><p>Offer three clues about a planned activity: it will be at home, it involves music, and comfortable clothes are welcome. Let the other person ask questions or decline. The surprise might be a living-room concert or a favorite-film night. Keeping the stakes low makes changing plans easier.</p><h2 id=\"real-world-decisions\">Know what should never be a surprise</h2><p>Do not surprise someone with sexual contact, public exposure, guests, recording, or activities that limit their ability to leave. These require specific prior agreement. A thoughtful surprise protects the other person's agency rather than treating their reaction as part of the entertainment.</p><h2 id=\"small-adjustments\">Small adjustments that matter</h2><p>Try anticipation that does not depend on secrecy: choose a day for a shared activity, send a clue about the meal, or invite a partner to pick the soundtrack. Ask whether surprises are enjoyable at all. If plans change, keep the invitation open rather than treating the change as a spoiled gift.</p>",
