    "id": "first-kiss-again",
    "title": "The First-Kiss-Again Date",
    "category": "Spicy articles",
    "art": "rose",
    "excerpt": "Rediscover affection without pretending you just met.",
    "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Long-term couples sometimes miss the nervous curiosity of early dates. You cannot recreate a first meeting, but you can choose to pay attention as though you still have things to learn.</p><h2 id=\"try\">Try it together</h2><p>Arrange a date with a fresh setting: a different café, a living-room picnic, or a walk somewhere familiar at a new time. Ask three questions you have never asked each other.</p><h2 id=\"example\">An everyday example</h2><p>One partner plans a surprise walk but checks whether the other wants an outing first. The anticipation comes from being considered, not being ambushed.</p><h2 id=\"care\">Consent and comfort</h2><p>Offer an affectionate goodbye or greeting only if both people want it. A kiss is an invitation, not the prize at the end of a successful date. Neither partner owes an explanation for declining or changing their mind.</p><h2 id=\"talk\">A conversation starter</h2><p>What would make a familiar date feel new without making it complicated?</p><h2 id=\"takeaway\">Make it your own</h2><p>Choose one part of the idea that fits your relationship and leave the rest. If you try it, check in afterward: did this feel enjoyable, neutral, or uncomfortable? Curiosity is useful even when the answer is 'not for us.' Respect privacy, accessibility needs, and differences in desire.</p><h2 id=\"practical-depth\">Putting the idea into practice</h2><h3>A first-date atmosphere without pretending</h3><p>Set aside the inside jokes for ten minutes and ask questions you would ask someone you genuinely wanted to know better. What are they excited about lately? What has changed their mind this year? Familiarity can make us assume we already know the answers. The point is not acting like strangers; it is making space for a partner to surprise you.</p><h3>What to avoid</h3><p>Do not spring a staged romantic scene on someone who dislikes surprises or public attention. Ask about the kind of evening they would enjoy, then keep the details flexible. A meaningful date can end with a warm goodbye, a laugh, or simply a plan for another conversation.</p>",
