    "id": "compliment-chemistry",
    "title": "Compliments With Chemistry: Beyond 'You Look Good'",
    "category": "Spicy articles",
    "art": "flame",
    "excerpt": "Specific attention can be more meaningful than grand declarations.",
    "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Compliments often land best when they show that someone noticed a choice, effort, or quality. Generic praise is fine, but specificity gives a partner something real to feel seen for.</p><h2 id=\"try\">Try it together</h2><p>Try naming a small action you appreciated, a creative decision you admired, or a moment when your partner made you laugh. Keep the focus on their experience rather than demanding a reaction.</p><h2 id=\"example\">An everyday example</h2><p>A partner says, 'I loved how you made our guests feel welcome,' rather than repeating the same appearance-based compliment.</p><h2 id=\"care\">Consent and comfort</h2><p>Avoid turning compliments into transactions or commenting on body features someone has said are sensitive. If praise makes a partner uncomfortable, ask what kind of acknowledgment feels better. Neither partner owes an explanation for declining or changing their mind.</p><h2 id=\"talk\">A conversation starter</h2><p>What is something about me you appreciate that I may not realize you notice?</p><h2 id=\"takeaway\">Make it your own</h2><p>Choose one part of the idea that fits your relationship and leave the rest. If you try it, check in afterward: did this feel enjoyable, neutral, or uncomfortable? Curiosity is useful even when the answer is 'not for us.' Respect privacy, accessibility needs, and differences in desire.</p><h2 id=\"practical-depth\">Putting the idea into practice</h2><h3>A three-part compliment experiment</h3><p>Choose one compliment about effort, one about character, and one about a shared memory. For example, notice the care someone put into a meal, the patience they showed a friend, or how they made a difficult day lighter. Give each compliment when it is true, not as a batch of scripted lines.</p><h3>Receiving praise can be complicated</h3><p>Some people find direct compliments uncomfortable or suspect they are a prelude to a request. If your partner brushes praise aside, do not argue them into accepting it. Ask whether quiet appreciation, written notes, or practical gestures feel more natural.</p>",
