    "id": "comfortable-closeness",
    "title": "Pillows, Blankets, and Better Cuddling",
    "category": "Spicy articles",
    "art": "rose",
    "excerpt": "Practical comfort is not the opposite of romance.",
    "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Comfort-focused closeness is easier when bodies can rest naturally. Support matters for necks, backs, knees, and anyone managing pain or limited mobility.</p><h2 id=\"try\">Try it together</h2><p>Try arranging pillows to support sitting or side-by-side rest. Ask whether your partner prefers shared blankets, separate blankets, or more personal space.</p><h2 id=\"example\">An everyday example</h2><p>A couple realizes that sharing one blanket causes constant tugging. Two blankets and one shared pillow make the evening much more enjoyable.</p><h2 id=\"care\">Consent and comfort</h2><p>Avoid pressure on the airway or positions that cause tingling, pain, or restricted breathing. Stop and change the setup when needed; do not treat discomfort as something to endure. Neither partner owes an explanation for declining or changing their mind.</p><h2 id=\"talk\">A conversation starter</h2><p>What tiny physical adjustment would make cuddling more comfortable?</p><h2 id=\"takeaway\">Make it your own</h2><p>Choose one part of the idea that fits your relationship and leave the rest. If you try it, check in afterward: did this feel enjoyable, neutral, or uncomfortable? Curiosity is useful even when the answer is 'not for us.' Respect privacy, accessibility needs, and differences in desire.</p><h2 id=\"practical-depth\">Putting the idea into practice</h2><h3>The cushion audit</h3><p>Ask each person where their body needs support before settling in. One may want an armrest, another a cushion behind the back, and another more room for their legs. Make adjustments early rather than waiting for discomfort to become distracting.</p><h3>Comfort includes freedom to move</h3><p>Some people feel confined by prolonged embraces, shared blankets, or being pinned against furniture. Agree that shifting position or stepping away does not need an apology. Connection is easier when neither person feels physically stuck.</p><h2 id=\"reader-takeaway\">One more way to make it work</h2><p>Before settling in, ask whether each person prefers warmth, weight, or personal space. Someone may like holding hands but dislike being under a shared blanket. Others enjoy leaning together for a few minutes and then moving apart. Treat these as preferences to accommodate rather than signals to interpret. Comfort can change even within one evening.</p><h2 id=\"editorial-deeper-dive\">A deeper dive</h2><h3>Think about support points</h3><p>A comfortable cuddle depends on where weight rests. If a shoulder is carrying another person's full weight, it may become uncomfortable quickly. Use pillows to support arms and necks, and give both people enough space to shift without disturbing the other. Side-by-side sitting can provide closeness without prolonged pressure.</p><h3>Permission to move is part of comfort</h3><p>Agree that either person can stretch, change sides, or separate without an apology. Some people prefer contact in short bursts rather than an entire movie. Heat, numbness, chronic pain, and sensory overload can change preferences mid-evening. The best position is the one people can leave easily and enjoy voluntarily.</p>",
    "practice": "What tiny physical adjustment would make cuddling more comfortable?",
