    "id": "flirty-questions",
    "title": "Twenty Flirty Questions That Aren't an Interrogation",
    "category": "Spicy articles",
    "art": "flame",
    "excerpt": "Curiosity is attractive when it leaves room to pass.",
    "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Playful questions can create anticipation, but they should not become a test of loyalty, openness, or sexual interest.</p><h2 id=\"try\">Try it together</h2><p>Start with light prompts: What outfit makes you feel most yourself? What kind of date would you plan with an unlimited afternoon? What gesture makes you feel noticed? Let answers lead the conversation.</p><h2 id=\"example\">An everyday example</h2><p>One partner asks what their ideal lazy Sunday looks like. The answer turns into a simple shared plan rather than a demand for more disclosure.</p><h2 id=\"care\">Consent and comfort</h2><p>Make skipping normal. Avoid questions designed to trap a partner into comparing bodies, revealing private history, or agreeing to an activity. Neither partner owes an explanation for declining or changing their mind.</p><h2 id=\"talk\">A conversation starter</h2><p>What is one question you wish I asked more often?</p><h2 id=\"takeaway\">Make it your own</h2><p>Choose one part of the idea that fits your relationship and leave the rest. If you try it, check in afterward: did this feel enjoyable, neutral, or uncomfortable? Curiosity is useful even when the answer is 'not for us.' Respect privacy, accessibility needs, and differences in desire.</p><h2 id=\"complete-question-list\">Twenty questions to choose from</h2><p>Choose only a few at a time. Anyone can skip a question without explanation; this is an invitation to connect, not a quiz.</p><ol><li>What is your favorite kind of compliment?</li><li>What would your ideal lazy date look like?</li><li>What song makes you want to dance?</li><li>What small gesture makes you smile?</li><li>Which outfit makes you feel most yourself?</li><li>What is your favorite memory of us laughing?</li><li>Where would you go for an imaginary weekend escape?</li><li>What food would you pick for a midnight picnic?</li><li>What is one affectionate nickname you actually like?</li><li>Would you choose a sunrise walk or a late-night movie?</li><li>What is the most thoughtful date someone could plan for you?</li><li>What is something new you want to learn together?</li><li>What does a perfect rainy afternoon look like?</li><li>What makes you feel noticed?</li><li>What is a funny first impression you had of me?</li><li>What would our imaginary theme song be?</li><li>What is a tradition we should invent?</li><li>Which fictional setting would make a great date?</li><li>What kind of surprise would be welcome?</li><li>What question would you like me to ask you next?</li></ol><h2 id=\"practical-depth\">Putting the idea into practice</h2><h3>A starter set of five</h3><p>Ask: What is your ideal unplanned afternoon? Which compliment has stayed with you? What ordinary activity feels romantic? What is a place you would love to visit together? What kind of attention makes you feel noticed? These prompts offer variety without asking anyone to disclose sexual details.</p><h3>Follow the answer, not the list</h3><p>If a question opens a good story, stay with it. A rapid-fire sequence can feel like an interview. Ask a follow-up, share your own answer if welcome, and let pauses happen. A good conversation rarely needs twenty questions in a row.</p><h2 id=\"editorial-deeper-dive\">A deeper dive</h2><h3>Choose questions that invite stories</h3><p>Try asking: Which compliment stayed with you? What tiny luxury makes a day better? What is the funniest date idea you would actually consider? What song reminds you of an adventure? What makes you feel seen when you're stressed? What would a perfect unplanned afternoon look like? What affectionate nickname would you never accept? Which memory deserves a sequel?</p><h3>Make room for the unexpected answer</h3><p>A question can lead to a serious memory even when it sounds playful. Listen without forcing the conversation back to flirtation. If your partner wants to skip a question, accept the pass. Do not turn responses into a scoreboard about who knows whom better. Save the prompts that spark laughter or genuine curiosity and discard the rest.</p>",
    "practice": "What is one question you wish I asked more often?",
    "tool": "conversation",
